{:edb/db-before-t 6145 :edb/db-after-t 6146 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539458 50 #inst "2026-10-24T02:16:56.934Z" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186138209 1071 "Bart bought $4$ tickets to go with his friends to the movie theater. If he paid $\\$12.69$ for each ticket, how much did Bart spend in total to buy the tickets?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186138209 1071 "Bart bought $4$ tickets to go with his friends to the movie theater. If he paid $\\[math]12.69$ for each ticket, how much did Bart spend in total to buy the tickets?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186138209 1074 "To find out how much Bart spent to buy the tickets, we need to multiply $\\$12.69$ by $4$.\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.6\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}4; 5\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nThen, we insert the decimal point in the result to have the same number of decimal places as the decimal factor.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $12.69$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ decimal places. We take our value of $5076$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n50\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{76}_{\\large\\text{\\color{blue}2 digits}}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n12.69 \\times 4 = 50.76 \\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, Bart spent $\\$50.76$ to buy the tickets." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186138209 1074 "To find out how much Bart spent to buy the tickets, we need to multiply $\\[math]12.69$ by $4$.\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.6\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}4; 5\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nThen, we insert the decimal point in the result to have the same number of decimal places as the decimal factor.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $12.69$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ decimal places. We take our value of $5076$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n50\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{76}_{\\large\\text{[/math]\\color{blue}2[math] digits}}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n12.69 \\times 4 = 50.76 \\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, Bart spent $\\[/math]50.76$ to buy the tickets." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186138212 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following multiplication problem?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 2 & [math]\\phantom{i}[/math]\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\phantom{i}[/math] \\mid 4\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186138212 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following multiplication problem?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 2 & \\phantom{i}\\end{vmatrix}\\phantom{i} \\mid 4\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186138212 1074 "First, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n_(5); 4 \\mid 7\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}6; 4 \\mid 9 \\cdot 6 = 54; Carry:5; Write:4\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\left._(4)\\right|_(5); 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix}7 \\cdot 6 = 42and5 + 42 = 47; Carry:4; Write:7\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the hundreds:\n\n$$\n\\left._(4)\\right|_(5); 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 2 & [math]\\color{red}8[/math]\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 4 \\mid 4 \\cdot 6 = 24and4 + 24 = 28; Carry:-; Write:28\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing digits, from left-to-right, are $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$ and $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186138212 1074 "First, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n_(5); 4 \\mid 7\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}6; 4 \\mid 9 \\cdot 6 = 54; Carry:5; Write:4\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\left._(4)\\right|_(5); 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix}7 \\cdot 6 = 42and5 + 42 = 47; Carry:4; Write:7\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the hundreds:\n\n$$\n\\left._(4)\\right|_(5); 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 2 & \\color{red}8\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 4 \\mid 4 \\cdot 6 = 24and4 + 24 = 28; Carry:-; Write:28\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing digits, from left-to-right, are $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$ and $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186138213 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following multiplication problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rrrrrr} &  & 1 & 8 & 5 & 2 \\\\ \\times &  &  &  &  & 7 \\\\ \\hline  & 1 & \\phantom{i} & 9 & \\phantom{i} & 4\\end{array}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186138213 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following multiplication problem?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\phantom{i}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}9 & [math]\\phantom{i}[/math]\\end{vmatrix}4\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186138213 1074 "First, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rrrrrr} &  &  &  & \\scriptstyle 1 &  \\\\  &  & 1 & 8 & 5 & 2 \\\\ \\times &  &  &  &  & 7 \\\\ \\hline  &  &  &  &  & 4\\end{array}\\qquad 2 \\times  7 = 14; \\quad\\text{Carry: }1; \\quad\\text{Write: }4\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rrrrrr} &  &  & \\scriptstyle 3 & \\scriptstyle 1 &  \\\\  &  & 1 & 8 & 5 & 2 \\\\ \\times &  &  &  &  & 7 \\\\ \\hline  &  &  &  & 6 & 4\\end{array}\\qquad 5 \\times  7 = 35\\quad\\text{and}\\quad 1 + 35 = 36; \\quad\\text{Carry: }3; \\quad\\text{Write: }6\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the hundreds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rrrrrr} &  & \\scriptstyle 5 & \\scriptstyle 3 & \\scriptstyle 1 &  \\\\  &  & 1 & 8 & 5 & 2 \\\\ \\times &  &  &  &  & 7 \\\\ \\hline  &  &  & 9 & 6 & 4\\end{array}\\qquad 8 \\times  7 = 56\\quad\\text{and}\\quad 3 + 56 = 59; \\quad\\text{Carry: }5; \\quad\\text{Write: }9\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the thousands:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rrrrrr} &  & \\scriptstyle 5 & \\scriptstyle 3 & \\scriptstyle 1 &  \\\\  &  & 1 & 8 & 5 & 2 \\\\ \\times &  &  &  &  & 7 \\\\ \\hline  & 1 & \\color{red}2 & 9 & 6 & 4\\end{array}\\qquad 1 \\times  7 = 7\\quad\\text{and}\\quad 5 + 7 = 12; \\quad\\text{Carry: }-; \\quad\\text{Write: }12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing digits, from left-to-right, are $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ and $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186138213 1074 "First, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n_(1); 1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}2 \\cdot 7 = 14; Carry:1; Write:4\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\left._(3)\\right|_(1); 1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 6\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 5 \\cdot 7 = 35and1 + 35 = 36; Carry:3; Write:6\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the hundreds:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\lef\\right|._(5) \\mid _(3)\\right|_(1); 1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 9\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 4 \\mid 8 \\cdot 7 = 56and3 + 56 = 59; Carry:5; Write:9\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the thousands:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\lef\\right|._(5) \\mid _(3)\\right|_(1); 1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\color{red}2[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix}1 \\cdot 7 = 7and5 + 7 = 12; Carry:-; Write:12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing digits, from left-to-right, are $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ and $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186138228 1071 "The table below shows the prices of five grocery items.\n\n|  | Price |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Bread | $\\$ 0.69$ 