By rounding the amount of juice to the nearest liter and the price per liter to the nearest dollar, find the amount of money Sophia will receive by selling all the juice." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186139079 1071 "Sophia wants to sell $7.55$ liters of orange juice at $\\$1.93$ per liter. By rounding the amount of juice to the nearest liter and the price per liter to the nearest dollar, find the amount of money Sophia will receive by selling all the juice." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186139080 1071 "An ice cream parlor bought $8.1$ kilograms of strawberries from a farmer. Each kilogram of strawberries had a cost of $$2.74$. By rounding the total weight of the strawberries to the nearest kilogram and the price per kilogram to the nearest dollar, how much money did the ice cream parlor pay the farmer?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186139080 1071 "An ice cream parlor bought $8.1$ kilograms of strawberries from a farmer. Each kilogram of strawberries had a cost of $\\$2.74$. By rounding the total weight of the strawberries to the nearest kilogram and the price per kilogram to the nearest dollar, how much money did the ice cream parlor pay the farmer?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186139604 1064 "The place value chart for the four-digit whole number $3,724$ is shown below:\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $7$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nThis place value chart has a new **thousands** column.\n\nFor this number:\n\n- the digit $3$ is in the **thousands place**\n- the digit $7$ is in the hundreds place\n- the digit $2$ is in the tens place\n- the digit $4$ is in the ones place\n\nThis tells us that $3,724$ is the same as $3$ thousands, plus $7$ hundreds, plus $2$ tens, plus $4$ ones.\n\nWhen writing down four-digit whole numbers, we separate the thousands and hundreds using a comma \"$$,\" to make it easier to read." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186139604 1064 "The place value chart for the four-digit whole number $3,724$ is shown below:\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $7$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nThis place value chart has a new **thousands** column.\n\nFor this number:\n\n- the digit $3$ is in the **thousands place**\n- the digit $7$ is in the hundreds place\n- the digit $2$ is in the tens place\n- the digit $4$ is in the ones place\n\nThis tells us that $3,724$ is the same as $3$ thousands, plus $7$ hundreds, plus $2$ tens, plus $4$ ones.\n\nWhen writing down four-digit whole numbers, we separate the thousands and hundreds using a comma \",\" to make it easier to read." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186142485 1071 "|  | $20$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $1$$4$$4$ |  |  |\n\nLaura paid $\\$144$ dollars for $6$ concert tickets. Using the box model above, find out how much Laura paid for each ticket." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186142485 1071 "|  | $20$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $1$$4$$4$ |  |  |\n\nLaura paid $\\[math]144$ dollars for $6$ concert tickets. Using the box model above, find out how much Laura paid for each ticket." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186142485 1074 "To find out how much Laura paid for each ticket, we need to divide $144$ by $6$.\n\nFirst, we subtract\n\n$$\n6 \\times {\\color{blue}{20}}=120\n$$\n\nfrom $144$.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}20$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $1$$4$$4$$-\\!$$1$$2$$0$$\\color{red}$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we bring $\\color{red}24$ to the right.\n\n|  | $20$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $1$$4$$4$$-\\!$$1$$2$$0$$\\color{red}$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}4$ | $\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!{\\color{red}2}$$\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\color{red}4$ |  |\n\nThen, we pick a multiple of $6$ that is easy to compute but is no larger than ${\\color{red}24}$. Let's pick\n\n$$\n6 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 24\