oy robot in a department store was $\\$75.$ The store manager decides to reduce the price by $12\\%.$ How much of a discount has the manager given?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186147850 1074 "To find the discount, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the old price, which is $$75.$ The percentage is $12%,$ and the part is the discount in dollars.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12%=12÷100=0.12\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12%\\times 75 \\\\  & =0.12\\times 75 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12%$ of $75$ is $9.$ So, the discount is $$9.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n12%=\\frac{12}{100}=\\frac{12÷4}{100÷4}=\\frac{3}{25}\n\n$$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12%\\times 75 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\times 75 \\\\  & =3\\times \\frac{75}{25} \\\\  & =3\\times 3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12%$ of $75$ is $9.$ So, the discount is $$9.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186147850 1074 "To find the discount, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the old price, which is $\\$75.$ The percentage is $12\\%,$ and the part is the discount in dollars.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12\\%=12÷100=0.12\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12\\%\\times 75 \\\\  & =0.12\\times 75 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12\\%$ of $75$ is $9.$ So, the discount is $\\$9.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n12\\%=\\frac{12}{100}=\\frac{12÷4}{100÷4}=\\frac{3}{25}\n\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12\\%\\times 75 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\times 75 \\\\  & =3\\times \\frac{75}{25} \\\\  & =3\\times 3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12\\%$ of $75$ is $9.$ So, the discount is $\\$9.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186147855 1071 "What is $30%$?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186147855 1071 "What is $30\\%$?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186147859 1071 "What is $300%$?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186147859 1071 "What is $300\\%$?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186147870 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the concrete mix, which is $600$ kilograms. The percentage is $19.5%,$ and the part is the weight of the crushed stone in the mix.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n19.5%=19.5÷100=0.195\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =19.5%\\times 600 \\\\  & =0.195\\times 600 \\\\  & =117\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.5%$ of $600$ is $117.$ Thus, there are $117$ kilograms of crushed stone in the concrete mix.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n19.5%=\\frac{19.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =19.5%\\times 600 \\\\  & =\\frac{19.5}{100}\\times 600 \\\\  & =19.5\\times \\frac{600}{100} \\\\  & =19.5\\times 6 \\\\  & =117\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.5%$ of $600$ is $117.$ Thus, there are $117$ kilograms of crushed stone in the concrete mix." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186147870 1074 "To solve this problem, we ca