\n$$\n22.4%=22.4÷100=0.224\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =22.4%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =0.224\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =336\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $22.4%$ of $1,500$ is $336.$ Thus, Margaret plans to spend $$336.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n22.4%=\\frac{22.4}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =22.4%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =\\frac{22.4}{100}\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =22.4\\times \\frac{1,500}{100} \\\\  & =22.4\\times 15 \\\\  & =336\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $22.4%$ of $1,500$ is $336.$ Thus, Margaret plans to spend $$336.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186147871 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of money Margaret has saved, which is $\\$1,500.$ The percentage is $22.4\\%,$ and the part is the amount of money that she decides to spend.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n22.4\\%=22.4÷100=0.224\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =22.4\\%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =0.224\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =336\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $22.4\\%$ of $1,500$ is $336.$ Thus, Margaret plans to spend $\\$336.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n22.4\\%=\\frac{22.4}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =22.4\\%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =\\frac{22.4}{100}\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =22.4\\times \\frac{1,500}{100} \\\\  & =22.4\\times 15 \\\\  & =336\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $22.4\\%$ of $1,500$ is $336.$ Thus, Margaret plans to spend $\\$336.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186147872 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of fabric Anna bought the first time, which is $15$ meters. The percentage is $160%,$ and the part is the amount of fabric she bought on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n160%=160÷100=1.6\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160%\\times 15 \\\\  & =1.6\\times 15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n160%=\\frac{160}{100}=\\frac{160÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160%\\times 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\times 15 \\\\  & =8\\times \\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =8\\times 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186147872 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of fabric Anna bought the first time, which is $15$ meters. The percentage is $160\\%,$ and the part is the amount of fabric she bought on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n160\\%=160÷100=1.6\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160\\%\\times 15 \\\\  & =1.6\\times 15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160\\%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n160\\%=\\frac{160}{100}=\\frac{160÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160\\%\\times 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\times 15 \\\\  & =8\\times \\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =8\\times 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160\\%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186147873 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of goals Fernando scored in the first season, which is $8$ goals. The percentage is $250%,$ and the part is the number of goals Fernando scored in the second season.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250%=250÷100=2.5\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =250%\\times 8 \\\\  & =2.5\\times 8 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $250%$ of $8$ is $20.$ So, Fernando scored $20$ goals in his second season.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n250%=\\frac{250}{100}=\\frac{250÷50}{100÷50}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =250%\\times 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\times 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $250%$ of $8$ is $20.$ So, Fernando scored $20$ goals in his second season." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186147873 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of goals Fernando scored in the first season, which is $8$ goals. The percentage is $250\\%,$ and the part is the number of 