n company.\n\nThe frequency table below represents the same information. From left to right, what is missing from the table?\n\n| Daily Income (\\$) | Number of Workers |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $000-000$ | $0$ |\n| $131-150$ | $8$ |\n| $151-170$ | $5$ |\n| $171-190$ | $3$ |" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186148070 1071 "The following frequency table shows the number of apps, grouped by price, sold by a computer programmer over a certain period. How many apps were sold for at least $$150$?\n\n| Price ($$$) | Number of Apps |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $50 - 99$ | $14$ |\n| $100 - 149$ | $9$ |\n| $150 - 199$ | $13$ |\n| $200 - 249$ | $10$ |" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186148070 1071 "The following frequency table shows the number of apps, grouped by price, sold by a computer programmer over a certain period. How many apps were sold for at least $\\$150$?\n\n| Price (\\$) | Number of Apps |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $50 - 99$ | $14$ |\n| $100 - 149$ | $9$ |\n| $150 - 199$ | $13$ |\n| $200 - 249$ | $10$ |" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186148110 1071 "Alex invests $$5000$ into stocks. The value $V(t)$ of his investment after $t$ years is given by\n\n$V(t) = 5000 \\cdot (1.3)^{t}$.\n\nWhat does the base of the exponential expression $(1.3)$ mean in this context?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186148110 1071 "Alex invests $\\$5000$ into stocks. The value $V(t)$ of his investment after $t$ years is given by\n\n$V(t) = 5000 \\cdot (1.3)^{t}$.\n\nWhat does the base of the exponential expression $(1.3)$ mean in this context?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186148699 1064 "Percentages are *very* common in everyday life. For example, they are used in retail, finance, and sports! In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate the percentage of a whole number in real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniel and Luisa traveled $200$ kilometers in their car. If Luisa drove $40 \\%$ of the way, how many kilometers did she drive?\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the whole is the number of kilometers traveled, which is $200$ kilometers. The percentage is $40 \\%,$ and the part is the distance Luisa drove.\n\nAs usual, we can use two methods to solve this problem:\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal: \n\n$$\n40 \\% = 40 \\div 100  = 0.4\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 200 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo Luisa drove $80$ kilometers.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n40 \\% = \\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 20}{100 \\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{5} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we find that Luisa drove $80$ kilometers." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186148699 1064 "Percentages are *very* common in everyday life. For example, they are used in retail, finance, and sports! In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate the percentage of a whole number in real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniel and Luisa traveled $200$ kilometers in their car. If Luisa drove $40 \\%$ of the way, how many kilometers did she drive?\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the whole is the number of kilometers traveled, which is $200$ kilometers. The percentage is $40 \\%,$ and the part is the distance Luisa drove.\n\nAs usual, we can use two methods to solve this problem:\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal: \n\n$$\n40 \\% = 40 \\div 100  = 0.4\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40\\%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 200 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo Luisa drove $80$ kilometers.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n40 \\% = \\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 20}{100 \\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40\\%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{5} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we find that Luisa drove $80$ kilometers." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186148781 1064 "To find a part given a percent and whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent} \\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part}\n$$\n\nHowever, we sometimes want to find a whole given a part and a percent. To do this, we can use the relationship between multiplication and division to rewrite the above formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percent}\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to find the missing number in the following statement:\n$$\n30\\% \\,\\,\\text{of}\\,\\, \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} \\,\\,\\text{is}\\,\\, 540\n$$\n\nIn this case, the part is $540$, and the percent is $30\\%.$ \n\nWe can express $30\\%$ as a fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30\\div 10}{100\\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nPutting these numbers into our formula for the whole, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =\\text{part}÷\\text{percent} \\\\  & =540÷30% \\\\  & =540÷\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =540\\times \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{540}{3}\\times 10 \\\\  & =180\\times 10 \\\\  & =1,800\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1,800.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186148781 1064 "To find a part given a percent and whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent