 amount of the markdown is $\\$18$. We subtract this amount from the price of the jacket:\n\n$$\n120 - 18 = 102\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dillon's total bill after the markdown is $\\$102$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186151925 1074 "First, we need to find the amount of the markdown. Then, we will subtract that amount from the price of the jacket.\n\nThe markdown is $15\\%$ of the price of the jacket, and the price of the jacket is $\\[math]120$. So, to calculate the amount of the markdown, we need to find $15\\%$ of $\\[/math]120$.\n\nTo do this, we first write $15\\%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n15\\% = 15 \\div 100 = 0.15\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $120$:\n\n$$\n0.15 \\times 120 = 18\n$$\n\nSo the amount of the markdown is $\\[math]18$. We subtract this amount from the price of the jacket:\n\n$$\n120 - 18 = 102\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dillon's total bill after the markdown is $\\[/math]102$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186151926 1071 "Cayden bought a shirt online. He requested that the shirt be shipped to him the very next day in exchange for a $40\\%$ markup on the price. If the final price of the shirt was $\\$35$, then what was the original price of the shirt?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186151926 1071 "Cayden bought a shirt online. He requested that the shirt be shipped to him the very next day in exchange for a $40\\%$ markup on the price. If the final price of the shirt was $\\[math]35$, then what was the original price of the shirt?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186151926 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent paid} \\times \\textrm{original price} = \\textrm{final price}\n$$\n\nThe markup was $40\\%$, so the percent that Cayden paid was\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nExpressing $140\\%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n140\\% = 140 \\div 100 = 1.4 \\,\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$35$. So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation which we can solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.4p &= 35 \\\\\np &= \\frac{35}{1.4} \\\\\np &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the shirt was $\\$25$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186151926 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent paid} \\times \\textrm{original price} = \\textrm{final price}\n$$\n\nThe markup was $40\\%$, so the percent that Cayden paid was\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nExpressing $140\\%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n140\\% = 140 \\div 100 = 1.4 \\,\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\[math]35$. So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation which we can solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.4p &= 35 \\\\\np &= \\frac{35}{1.4} \\\\\np &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the shirt was $\\[/math]25$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186151927 1071 "Meredith bought a lawnmower on sale for $\\$150$. If the markdown was $20\\%$, what was the original price of the lawnmower?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186151927 1071 "Meredith bought a lawnmower on sale for $\\[math]150$. If the markdown was $20\\%$, what was the original price of the lawnmower?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186151927 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent paid} \\times \\textrm{original price} = \\textrm{final price}\n$$\n\nThe markdown was $20\\%$, so the percent that Meredith paid was\n\n$$\n100\\% - 20\\% = 80\\%\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$150$. So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation which we can solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.8p &= 150 \\\\\np &= \\frac{150}{0.8} \\\\\np &= 187.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the lawnmower was $\\$187.50$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186151927 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent paid} \\times \\textrm{original price} = \\textrm{final price}\n$$\n\nThe markdown was 