aving a typo. What is the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences since the previous typo? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186157867 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $15%,$ the number of sentences since the previous typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=15%=\\frac{15}{100}=0.15.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.15)^{x-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{x-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{1-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{2-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.15)+(0.85)(0.15)+(0.85)^{2}(0.15) \\\\  & =[1+0.85+(0.85)^{2}](0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.386\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186157867 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $15\\%,$ the number of sentences since the previous typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=15\\%=\\frac{15}{100}=0.15.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.15)^{x-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{x-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{1-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{2-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.15)+(0.85)(0.15)+(0.85)^{2}(0.15) \\\\  & =[1+0.85+(0.85)^{2}](0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.386\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186157869 1071 "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $5%$ chance of containing a typo. What is the probability of finding the first typo in the $4$th sentence or later? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186157869 1071 "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $5\\%$ chance of containing a typo. What is the probability of finding the first typo in the $4$th sentence or later? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186157869 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $5%,$ the number of sentences checked until the first typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=5%=\\frac{5}{100}=0.05.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.05).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.05)^{x-1}(0.05) \\\\  & =(0.95)^{x-1}(0.05)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require $P(X\\ge 4).$ \n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X<4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.95)^{1-1}(0.05)+(0.95)^{2-1}(0.05)+(0.95)^{3-1}(0.05) \\\\  & =(0.05)+(0.95)(0.05)+(0.95)^{2}(0.05) \\\\  & =[1+0.95+(0.95)^{2}](0.05) \\\\  & ≈0.143,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, there are no typos within the first three sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & ≈1-0.143 \\\\  & ≈0.857\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186157869 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $5\\%,$ the number of sentences checked until the first typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=5\\%=\\frac{5}{100}=0.05.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.05).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.05)^{x-1}(0.05) \\\\  & =(0.95)^{x-1}(0.05)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require $P(X\\ge 4).$ \n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X<4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.95)^{1-1}(0.05)+(0.95)^{2-1}(0.05)+(0.95)^{3-1}(0.05) \\\\  & =(0.05)+(0.95)(0.05)+(0.95)^{2}(0.05) \\\\  & =[1+0.95+(0.95)^{2}](0.05) \\\\  & ≈0.143,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, there are no typos within the first three sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & ≈1-0.143 \\\\  & ≈0.857\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186158434 1064 "Suppose $J_n(\\lambda)$ is an $n\\times n$ Jordan block where the main diagonal entries are all $\\lambda{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 1 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & \\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor a scalar function $f$ analytic at $\\lambda$ (i.e., has a Taylor series expansion at $\\lambda$), $f(J_n(\\lambda))$ is the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{''}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $f(J_3(2))$ for $f(x) = \\ln x.$\n\nSince $J_3(2)$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, we will need the first $3-1=2$ derivatives of $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}x \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{''}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\ln(J_3(2))\n\n= f(J_3(2))\n\n\n\n\n\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}f ( 2 ) & {f}^{\\prime } ( 2 ) & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2 !}} {f}^{\\prime\\prime } ( 2 ) \\\\ 0 & f ( 2 ) & {f}^{\\prime } ( 2 ) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f ( 2 )\\end{array} \\right]}\n\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}\\ln   2 & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{8}} \\\\ 0 & \\ln   2 & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\ln   2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nNow that we can compute the value of a function of a Jordan block, we can also find the value of a function of a Jordan matrix.\n\nThe matrix $f(J_n(\\lambda))$ for $n=2,3,$ and $4$ are as follows.\n\n* For $2\\times 2$ Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_2(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n* For $3\\times 3$ Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_3(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & 0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n* For $4\\times 4$ Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_4(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) & \\dfrac16f'''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambd