ur answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186162563 1071 "Last week, Thumper's ice cream parlor achieved median sales of $$140$ per day. The total daily sales of Bambi's ice cream parlor over the same $7$ day period are as follows:\n$$120, $135, $135, $140, $145, $150, $170$\nWhich of the following statement are true?\n\n1. Bambi's ice cream parlor achieved median sales of $$140$ per day.\n2. The median sales achieved by Thumper's parlor exceed that of Bambi's.\n3. A comparison of the medians suggests that Thumper's store is more popular overall with customers wanting ice cream.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186162563 1071 "Last week, Thumper's ice cream parlor achieved median sales of $\\$140$ per day. The total daily sales of Bambi's ice cream parlor over the same $7$ day period are as follows:\n$\\$120, $135, $135, $140, $145, $150, $170$\nWhich of the following statement are true?\n\n1. Bambi's ice cream parlor achieved median sales of $\\$140$ per day.\n2. The median sales achieved by Thumper's parlor exceed that of Bambi's.\n3. A comparison of the medians suggests that Thumper's store is more popular overall with customers wanting ice cream.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186162573 1071 "Company $A$ pays a modal starting salary of $$40000$ per year. The annual salaries of the currently advertised positions of Company $B$ are as follows:\n$$35000, $40000, $40000, $42000, $45000$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The modal starting salary of Company $B$ is $$40000$.\n2. The modal starting salaries of the two companies are equal.\n3. A comparison of the modal salaries suggests that Company $A$ pays a greater starting salary than Company $B$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186162573 1071 "Company $A$ pays a modal starting salary of $\\$40000$ per year. The annual salaries of the currently advertised positions of Company $B$ are as follows:\n$\\$35000, $40000, $40000, $42000, $45000$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The modal starting salary of Company $B$ is $\\$40000$.\n2. The modal starting salaries of the two companies are equal.\n3. A comparison of the modal salaries suggests that Company $A$ pays a greater starting salary than Company $B$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186163300 1064 "Let's once again consider the line integral with respect to arc length of $f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nThere are two more important properties of this type of line integral that we need to be aware of.\n\n- There is usually more than one way to parametrize a curve. However, line integrals with respect to arc length are *independent of the choice of parametrization*. In other words, we get the same result regardless of which parametrization we choose.\n\nFor example, the paths $C_1$ and $C_2,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}t⟩, & 0\\le t<2\\pi  \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}2u,\\,\\operatorname{sin}2u⟩, & 0\\le u<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nboth traverse a circle of radius $1$ once in the counterclockwise direction starting from $(1,0).$ However, since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of parametrization, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\n- Line integrals with respect to arc length are also *independent of the orientation* of $C.$ For example, suppose that $C$ is a path along a particular curve traversed from the point $A$ to the point $B.$ Then, $-C$ is a path along the same curve but traversed from $B$ to $A,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4f68e10429fa4bda69366751c303216d27df995edf2502b61ab9bfe84c3ff45.png)\n\nIndependence of orientation means that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{-C} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- Finally, it's important to mention that all of the properties discussed in this lesson apply to line integrals with respect to arc length over space curves, i.e., line integrals of the form $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate point 2 above, consider the line integral\n\\[\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s, \n\\]\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $A(0,0)$ to $B(2,2).$ \n\nOne possible parameterization of $C$ from $A$ to $B$ is $\\mathbf r = \\langle t, t\\rangle$ for $t\\in[0,2].$ We get $x=y=t$ on the curve, and $\\mathbf r ' = \\langle 1, 1\\rangle$ which leads to $||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.$ Therefore, compuing the line integral gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 t\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we *reverse* the direction of the curve, traversing from $B$ to $A,$ we get the parametrization \n\\[\n\\mathbf r(0+2-t) = \\mathbf r(2-t) =   \\langle 2-t,2- t\\rangle,\\quad t \\in[0,2].\n\\]\nNow, we get $x=y=2-t, \\mathbf r' = \\langle -1, -1\\rangle$ and so $||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.$ Computing the line integral using this parametrization gives\n\\[\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 (2-t)\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n\\]" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186163300 1064 "Let's once again consider the line integral with respect to arc length of $f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nThere are two more important properties of this type of line integral that we need to be aware of.\n\n- There is usually more than one way to parametrize a curve. However, line integrals with respect to arc length are *independent of the choice of parametrization*. In other words, we get the same result regardless of which parametrization we choose.\n\nFor example, the paths $C_1$ and $C_2,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}t⟩, & 0\\le t<2\\pi  \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}2u,\\,\\operatorname{sin}2u⟩, & 0\\le u<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nboth traverse a circle of radius $1$ once in the counterclockwise direction starting from $(1,0).$ However, since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of parametrization, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\n- Line integrals with respect to arc length are also *independent of the orientation* of $C.$ For example, suppose that $C$ is a path along a particular curve traversed from the point $A$ to the point $B.$ Then, $-C$ is a path along the same curve but traversed from $B$ to $A,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4f68e10429fa4bda69366751c303216d27df995edf2502b61ab9bfe84c3ff45.png)\n\nIndependence of orientation means that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{-C} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- Finally, it's important to mention that all of the properties discussed in this lesson apply to line integrals with respect to arc length over space curves, i.e., line integrals of the form $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate point 2 above, consider the line integral\n\\[\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s, \n\\]\nwhere [math]C[/math] is the line segment from [math]A(0,0)[/math] to [math]B(2,2).[/math] \n\nOne possible parameterization of [math]C[/math] from [math]A[/math] to [math]B[/math] is [math]\\mathbf r = \\langle t, t\\rangle[/math] for [math]t\\in[0,2].[/math] We get [math]x=y=t[/math] on the curve, and [math]\\mathbf r ' = \\langle 1, 1\\rangle[/math] which leads to [math]||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.[/math] Therefore, compuing the line integral gives\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 t\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n\\end{align*}\nIf we *reverse* the direction of the curve, traversing from [math]B[/math] to [math]A,[/math] we get the parametrization \n\\[\n\\mathbf r(0+2-t) = \\mathbf r(2-t) =   \\langle 2-t,2- t\\rangle,\\quad t \\in[0,2].\n\\]\nNow, we get [math]x=y=2-t, \\mathbf r' = \\langle -1, -1\\rangle[/math] and so [math]||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.[/math] Computing the line integral using this parametrization gives\n\\[\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 (2-t)\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n\\]" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186163371 1064 "Suppose we have a coin that we suspect is biased *against* landing on heads (i.e., the coin is *less* likely to land on heads than tails). We wish to design a hypothesis test to determine whether or not our suspicions are correct.\n\nWe can use a so-called **left-tailed** hypothesis test for this purpose. To do that, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Determine the population parameter we're interested in. $\\qquad$ *We define $p$ as \"the probability that the coin lands on heads when tossed randomly.\"*\n- **Step 2**: Define our hypotheses:*Since we want to check whether the coin is biased **against** landing on heads, we have the following null and alternative hypotheses:*\n$$\n\\displaystyle H_{0}: p|= \\frac{1}{2}; H_{1}: p \\mid < \\frac{1}{2}\n$$We use a \"less than\" symbol (the left tail of the probability distribution) for $H_1$ because if the coin is biased against heads, then the probability that the coin lands on heads must be less than one-half.\n- **Step 3**: Design a suitable experiment to test our hypotheses. $\\qquad$ *We toss our coin $10$ times and count the total number of heads.*\n- **Step 4**: Set a significance level $\\alpha$. $\\qquad$ *For our experiment, let's pick the significance level $\\alpha = 5\\%$.*\n- **Step 5**: Define and calculate a test statistic.In our case, we might define a suitable test statistic as follows:$\\qquad X=$ \"*the number of times the coin lands on heads after $10$ random tosses*.\"Notice that under the conditions specified in the null hypothesis, the random variable $X$ must follow a binomial distribution:\n$X \\sim B\\left(10,\\dfrac12\\right)$ Suppose we toss the coin $10$ times and get only ${\\color{red}{1}}$ head. If the null hypothesis is true, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(X \\le 1)|= P(X = 0) + P(X = 1); = \\left(\\frac{10}{0}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{0}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{10} + \\left(\\frac{10}{1}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{9}; \\approx 0.0107; = 1.07%.\n$$\n- **Step 6**: Draw a conclusion.The probability $P(X\\leq {\\color{red}{1}})$ is *smaller than* our significance level of $5\\%$. This means that our result *is* statistically significant. In other words, it is unlikely that this event could have occurred if the null hypothesis is true. Therefore, we should *reject* the null hypothesis and conclude that the coin is biased.\n\nYou might be wondering why our test statistic was\n\n$$\nP(X\\leq {\\color{red}{1}})\n$$\n\nrather than\n\n$$\nP(X={\\color{red}{1}})\n$$\n\nWhen conducting hypothesis tests, we consider not just the observed outcome, but all outcomes that are *at least as extreme*—meaning outcomes that point even more strongly toward the alternative hypothesis. Since our alternative hypothesis is\n\n$$\np < \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nlower values of $X$ provide stronger evidence for it. The cumulative probability\n\n$$\nP(X\\leq 1)\n$$\n\naccounts for $X = 1$ and the even more extreme outcome $X = 0$.\n\n**Right-tailed** hypothesis tests are similar. The difference is the alternative hypothesis involves a \"greater than\" sign. Let's see an example." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186163371 1064 "Suppose we have a coin that we suspe