he blanks to complete the weighted-average formula for the increment and the stage definitions, starting at the point $(x,y) .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & =f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})\\,\\text{ where }\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\boxed{\\text{\\phantom{(q_0,1,L)}}},\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & =f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})\\,\\text{ where }\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,\\boxed{\\text{\\phantom{(q_0,1,L)}}}) \\\\ {y}_{3}^{′} & =f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})\\,\\text{ where }\\,({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(\\boxed{\\text{\\phantom{(q_0,1,L)}}},\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186166582 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event that they tested positive. Then, we require $P(D'|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(D'|T) = \\dfrac{P(T|D')P(D')}{P(T)}= \\dfrac{P(T|D')P(D')}{P(T|D) P(D) + P(T | D') P(D')}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability that an infected person tests positive is $94\\%,$ and the probability that a non-infected person tests positive is $2\\%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|D)=0.94, \\quad P(T|D')=0.02.\n$$\n\n- Since only $5\\%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.05, \\quad P(D') = 1 - 0.05 = 0.95.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|T) & =\\frac{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P(T|D)P(D)+P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.02)(0.95)}{(0.94)(0.05)+(0.02)(0.95)} \\\\  & ≈0.288 \\\\  & ≈29%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When a non-infected patient tests positive for a disease, we call this a **false positive.**\n\n- When an infected patient tests negative for a disease, we call this a **false negative.**" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186166582 