ac{P(F|B)P(B)}{P(F)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(F|B)=0.9,$ since $90%$ of all people who have a bachelor's degree speak a foreign language.\n\n- $P(B)=0.31,$ since $31%$ of the adult population has a bachelor's degree.\n\n- $P(F)=0.63,$ since $63%$ of the adult population speak a foreign language.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|F) & =\\frac{P(F|B)P(B)}{P(F)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.9)(0.31)}{0.63} \\\\  & ≈44%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186167057 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a randomly selected person speaks a foreign language, and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected person has a bachelor's degree. Then, we require $P(B|F),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=\\frac{P(F|B)P(B)}{P(F)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(F|B)=0.9,$ since $90\\%$ of all people who have a bachelor's degree speak a foreign language.\n\n- $P(B)=0.31,$ since $31\\%$ of the adult population has a bachelor's degree.\n\n- $P(F)=0.63,$ since $63\\%$ of the adult population speak a foreign language.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|F) & =\\frac{P(F|B)P(B)}{P(F)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.9)(0.31)}{0.63} \\\\  & ≈44\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186167058 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $95%$ of those infected and $2%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $20%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is infected?  Round your answer to four decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186167058 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $95\\%$ of those infected and $2\\%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $20\\%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is infected?  Round your answer to four decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186167058 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(D|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(D|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $95%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $2%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.95=0.05,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.02=0.98.\n$$\n\n- Since only $20%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.2\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.2=0.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.05)(0.2)}{(0.05)(0.2)+(0.98)(0.8)} \\\\  & ≈0.0126\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186167058 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(D|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(D|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $95\\%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $2\\%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.95=0.05,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.02=0.98.\n$$\n\n- Since only $20\\%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.2\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.2=0.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.05)(0.2)}{(0.05)(0.2)+(0.98)(0.8)} \\\\  & ≈0.0126\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186167117 1071 "The students in a particular school are split among three classes, class $1,$ class $2,$ and class $3.$\n\n- Class $1$ contains $25%$ of the students in the school, and $55%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $2$ contains $35%$ of the students in the school, and $40%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $3$ contains $40%$ of the students in the school.\n\nIf $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, $80%$ of female students take biology, and $70%$ of biology students are female, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from class $3$ takes biology? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186167117 1071 "The students in a particular school are split among three classes, class $1,$ class $2,$ and class $3.$\n\n- Class $1$ contains $25\\%$ of the students in the school, and $55\\%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $2$ contains $35\\%$ of the students in the school, and $40\\%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $3$ contains $40\\%$ of the students in the school.\n\nIf $45\\%$ of students in the entire school are female, $80\\%$ of female students take biology, and $70\\%$ of biology students are female, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from class $3$ takes biology? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186167117 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $B$ be the event that a student takes biology, and ${C}_{1},{C}_{2},{C}_{3}$ be the event that a student is in class $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({C}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{C}_{1})=0.55 \\\\  & P({C}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(B|{C}_{2})=0.4 \\\\  & P({C}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})+P({C}_{2})+P({C}_{3})=0.25+0.35+0.4=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females take biology, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of biology students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|B)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student from class $3$ takes biology, which is represented as $P(B|{C}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.45)}{(0.55)(0.25)+(0.4)(0.35)+P(B|{C}_{3})(0.4)} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{0.36}{0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{C}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.7(0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})) & =0.36 \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.36}{0.7} \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35} \\\\ 0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775 \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.4}(\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775) \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & ≈0.592\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186167117 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $B$ be the event that a student takes biology, and ${C}_{1},{C}_{2},{C}_{3}$ be the event that a student is in class $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the probl