58 17592186576411 false] [17592186167464 1071 "Find a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186167464 1074 "$$\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x\\,-\\,{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186167464 1074 "$$\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x\\,-\\,{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186167465 1071 "$$\nFind a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186167465 1071 "Find a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186167465 1074 "$$\n\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\n" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186167465 1074 "$$\n\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186167516 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of correct answers in a multiple choice test of $10$ questions, where each answer is either \"true\" or \"false,\" and each answer is guessed randomly” cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each number of correct answers is not equally likely. For example, it is more likely to score $50%$ than $100%.$\n\n- Random variable “The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel” can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. For example, each outcome of a roulette wheel is represented by a number between $0$ and $37,$ and since the roulette is fair, each number is equally likely.\n\n- Random variable “The initial letter of a word picked at random from an English dictionary” cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because some letters are more common than others.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel” only." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186167516 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of correct answers in a multiple choice test of $10$ questions, where each answer is either \"true\" or \"false,\" and each answer is guessed randomly” cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each number of correct answers is not equally likely. For example, it is more likely to score $50\\%$ than $100\\%.$\n\n- Random variable “The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel” can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. For example, each outcome of a roulette wheel is represented by a number between $0$ and $37,$ and since the roulette is fair, each number is equally likely.\n\n- Random variable “The initial letter of a word picked at random from an English dictionary” cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because some letters are more common than others.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel” only." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186167999 1064 "How do local errors build to grow into global errors?\n\nAgain, suppose a numerical method advances the solution using the increment formula\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\Phi(x, y, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nfor some function $\\Phi.$\n\n- LTE $\\tau_n$ measures the error made in *one step*, where the step begins from the exact value $y(x_{n-1}).$\n\n- GTE $e_n$ measures the accumulated error after *many steps*, starting from $x_0$ and using the numerical method recursively.\n\nSo, at each step, we can think of the GTE as a combination of\n\n- the total accumulated error from the previous steps ($e_{n-1}$),\n\n- the way the method propagates these errors forward, and\n\n- a new error introduced in the current step ($\\tau_n$).\n\nIn particular, the global truncation error satisfies the following:\n\n$$\ne_n = e_{n-1} + \\left[ \\Phi(x_{n-1}, y(x_{n-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{n-1}, y_{n-1}, \\Delta x)\\right] \\cdot \\Delta x + \\tau_n\n$$\n\nIndeed, using the iterative notation, at step $n,$ the local truncation error is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & =y({x}_{n})-(y({x}_{n-1})+Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)\\cdot Δx).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging, we get an expression for the exact value $y(x_n){:}$\n\n$$\ny(x_n) = y(x_{n-1}) + \\Phi(x_{n-1}, y(x_{n-1}), \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x + \\tau_n\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this expression into the equation for GTE, we obtain our recursion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{n} & =y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n} \\\\  & =y({x}_{n})-({y}_{n-1}+Δ{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =[y({x}_{n-1})+Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)\\cdot Δx+{τ}_{n}]-[{y}_{n-1}+Φ({x}_{n-1},{y}_{n-1},Δx)\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =(y({x}_{n-1})-{y}_{n-1})+(Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)-Φ({x}_{n-1},{y}_{n-1},Δx))\\cdot Δx+{τ}_{n} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{{e}_{n-1}}_{\\text{GTE at step [math]n-1[/math]}}+\\underbrace{[Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)-Φ({x}_{n-1},{y}_{n-1},Δx)]\\cdot Δx}_{\\text{propagation of }{e}_{n-1}}+\\underbrace{{τ}_{n}}_{\\text{LTE at step [math]n[/math]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagram below illustrates this propagation, where $\\Delta y_n$ is the increment produced by the numerical method at step $n$ starting from the previous numerical value $y_{n-1},$ while $\\Delta y$ is the increment produced by the same formula but starting from the exact value $y(x_{n-1}).$\n\n![](images/c6/c622e0a973f930cd91df87aad702c1b893f94559c599685a2ca93d4d222b4fc3.png)" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186167999 1064 "How do local errors build to grow into global errors?\n\nAgain, suppose a numerical method advances the solution using the increment formula\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\Phi(x, y, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nfor some function $\\Phi.$\n\n- LTE $\\tau_n$ measures the error made in *one step*, where the step begins from the exact value $y(x_{n-1}).$\n\n- GTE $e_n$ measures the accumulated error after *many steps*, starting from $x_0$ and using the numerical method recursively.\n\nSo, at each step, we can think of the GTE as a combination of\n\n- the total accumulated error from the previous steps ($e_{n-1}$),\n\n- the way the method propagates these errors forward, and\n\n- a new error introduced in the current step ($\\tau_n$).\n\nIn particular, the global truncation error satisfies the following:\n\n$$\ne_n = e_{n-1} + \\left[ \\Phi(x_{n-1}, y(x_{n-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{n-1}, y_{n-1}, \\Delta x)\\right] \\cdot \\Delta x + \\tau_n\n$$\n\nIndeed, using the iterative notation, at step $n,$ the local truncation error is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & =y({x}_{n})-(y({x}_{n-1})+Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)\\cdot Δx).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging, we get an expression for the exact value $y(x_n){:}$\n\n$$\ny(x_n) = y(x_{n-1}) + \\Phi(x_{n-1}, y(x_{n-1}), \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x + \\tau_n\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this expression into the equation for GTE, we obtain our recursion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{n} & =y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n} \\\\  & =y({x}_{n})-({y}_{n-1}+Δ{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =[y({x}_{n-1})+Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)\\cdot Δx+{τ}_{n}]-[{y}_{n-1}+Φ({x}_{n-1},{y}_{n-1},Δx)\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =(y({x}_{n-1})-{y}_{n-1})+(Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)-Φ({x}_{n-1},{y}_{n-1},Δx))\\cdot Δx+{τ}_{n} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{{e}_{n-1}}_{\\text{GTE at step n-1}}+\\underbrace{[Φ({x}_{n-1},y({x}_{n-1}),Δx)-Φ({x}_{n-1},{y}_{n-1},Δx)]\\cdot Δx}_{\\text{propagation of }{e}_{n-1}}+\\underbrace{{τ}_{n}}_{\\text{LTE at step n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagram below illustrates this propagation, where $\\Delta y_n$ is the increment produced by the numerical method at step $n$ starting from the previous numerical value $y_{n-1},$ while $\\Delta y$ is the increment produced by the same formula but starting from the exact value $y(x_{n-1}).$\n\n![](images/c6/c622e0a973f930cd91df87aad702c1b893f94559c599685a2ca93d4d222b4fc3.png)" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186171722 1071 "Consider the infinite list of real decimal numbers from the interval $(1,2)$, as shown below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}Number 1: & 1.a_{11}a_{12}…a_{1n}… \\\\ Number 2: & 1.a_{21}a_{22}…a_{2n}… \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ Number [math]n[/math]: & 1.a_{n1}a_{n2}…a_{nn}… \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nConstruct a new number from $(1,2)$ that is guaranteed to be different from each of the given numbers on the list." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186171722 1071 "Consider the infinite list of real decimal numbers from the interval $(1,2)$, as shown below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}Number 1: & 1.a_{11}a_{12}…a_{1n}… \\\\ Number 2: & 1.a_{21}a_{22}…a_{2n}… \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ Number n: & 1.a_{n1}a_{n2}…a_{nn}… \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nConstruct a new number from $(1,2)$ that is guaranteed to be different from each of the given numbers on the list." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186171738 1074 "According to the definition, \n$$\n7^4 = \\underbrace{7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7}_{\\large{ {4} \\text{ times}}}.\n$$\n\nSince $7 \\times 7 = 49,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\times 7}_{\\color{blue}49} \\times \\underbrace{7 \\times 7}_{\\color{blue}49} = {\\color{blue}49}