error}}\n$$\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ ((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ ((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{x}^{2}}{{n}_{x}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{y}^{2}}{{n}_{y}}}, & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{x}^{2}}{{n}_{x}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{y}^{2}}{{n}_{y}}}\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186173242 1064 "Suppose we have independent random samples of sizes $n_x$ and $n_y$ from normal populations with unknown population means $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ and *known* population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2.$ \n\nFor a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_x - \\mu_y$ between the population means is given by\n\n$$\n\\left((\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}, \\: (\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}} \\: \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$ \n\nThe confidence limits (i.e., endpoints of our interval) are given by\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) \\: \\pm \\: z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}.\n$$\n\nAs usual, each part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}$ is an estimate of the difference of the population means\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}\n$$ is the standard error\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E = z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}\n$$ is the margin of error\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{blue}(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})}_{\\textrm{estimate}} \\:\\: \\pm \\:\\: \\overbrace{ \\underbrace{z_{\\alpha/2}}_{ { {z} \\textrm{-score}}} \\:\\: \\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}}_{\\textrm{standard} \\\\ \\:\\:\\:\\textrm{error}} }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ ((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-[\\text{z-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+[\\text{z-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ ((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{x}^{2}}{{n}_{x}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{y}^{2}}{{n}_{y}}}, & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{x}^{2}}{{n}_{x}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{y}^{2}}{{n}_{y}}}\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186175743 1071 "To produce a certain brand of t-shirts, a manufacturing company bases the price of each unit on the price function $p(x) = 50-0.1x$, where $x$ represents the number of units produced and sold per month. Each unit costs $\\$20$ to make, and the company’s fixed costs are $\\$2\\,000$ per month. Calculate the profit function $P(x)$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186175743 1071 "To produce a certain brand of t-shirts, a manufacturing company bases the price of each unit on the price function $p(x) = 50-0.1x$, where $x$ represents the number of units produced and sold per month. Each unit costs $\\[math]20$ to make, and the company’s fixed costs are $\\[/math]2\\,000$ per month. Calculate the profit function $P(x)$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186175743 1074 "We know that the profit function is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=R(x)-C(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of units, $C(x)$ is the cost function and $R(x)$ is the revenue function.\n\nWe're given the price function $p(x)$, so the revenue function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot p(x) \\\\\n&= x(50 - 0.1x) \\\\\n&= 50x - 0.1x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that the fixed costs are $\\$2\\,000$ per month, and each unit costs $\\$20$ to make. Therefore, the cost function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= (fixed cost) + (number of units) \\cdot (cost per unit) \\\\\n&= 2000 + x \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 2000 + 20x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now calculate the profit function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (50x - 0.1x^{2}) - (2000 + 20x) \\\\\n&= 50x - 0.1x^{2} - 2000 - 20x \\\\\n&=-0.1x^{2} + 30x - 2000\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186175743 1074 "We know that the profit function is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=R(x)-C(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of units, $C(x)$ is the cost function and $R(x)$ is the revenue function.\n\nWe're given the price function $p(x)$, so the revenue function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot p(x) \\\\\n&= x(50 - 0.1x) \\\\\n&= 50x - 0.1x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that the fixed costs are $\\[math]2\\,000$ per month, and each unit costs $\\[/math]20$ to make. Therefore, the cost function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= (fixed cost) + (number of units) \\cdot (cost per unit) \\\\\n&= 2000 + x \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 2000 + 20x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now calculate the profit function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (50x - 0.1x^{2}) - (2000 + 20x) \\\\\n&= 50x - 0.1x^{2} - 2000 - 20x \\\\\n&=-0.1x^{2} + 30x - 2000\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186175744 1074 "In general, the minimum cost corresponds to the lowest point on the graph of $C(x)$.\n\n![](images/cc/cc89cf0c8f2d0ec92d1b3cff58f8bf5cf5d500ae4ca5488e573879d0b3658c3e.png)\n\nIn this case, the cost function\n\n$$\nC(x)=0.4x^2-24x+520\n$$\n\nis a parabola that opens upward (since the leading coefficient is positive). The lowest point on the graph of $C(x)$ is the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nx_{\\textrm{min}}= -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nC(x) =0.4x^2-24x+520\n$$\n\nwe have $a = 0.4$ and $b=-24$, which gives\n\n$$\nx_{\\textrm{min}}=-\\dfrac{-24}{2(0.4)}=30\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=30$ into the cost function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(30) &= 0.4(30)^{2} - 24(30) + 520 \\\\\n&= 360 - 720 + 520 \\\\\n&= 160\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible weekly cost is\n\n$$\n160 \\cdot100 = \\$16\\,000\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186175744 1074 "In general, the minimum cost corresponds to the lowest point on the graph of $C(x)$.\n\n![](images/cc/cc89cf0c8f2d0ec92d1b3cff58f8bf5cf5d500ae4ca5488e573879d0b3658c3e.png)\n\nIn this case, the cost function\n\n$$\nC(x)=0.4x^2-24x+520\n$$\n\nis a parabola that opens upward (since the leading coefficient is positive). The lowest point on the graph of $C(x)$ is the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nx_{\\textrm{min}}= -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nC(x) =0.4x^2-24x+520\n$$\n\nwe have $a = 0.4$ and $b=-24$, which gives\n\n$$\nx_{\\textrm{min}}=-\\dfrac{-24}{2(0.4)}=30\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=30$ into the cost function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(30) &= 0.4(30)^{2} - 24(30) + 520 \\\\\n&= 360 - 720 + 520 \\\\\n&= 160\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible weekly cost is\n\n$$\n160 \\cdot100 = \\[math]16\\,000\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186175860 1074 "Recall that $F(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/18/181df1b4d320fac30e8fc774975c8e1ae3fa6f4d360a49a38d23f1de96ebf7cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,5).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $\\color{red}(5,2)$ and ${\\color{red}{(0,5)}}.$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ${\\color{blue}{(0,2)}}.$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 5,2\\le Y\\le 5) & =\\overbrace{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 5)}^{\\text{top-right corner at (5,5)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 2)}^{\\text{top-right corner at (5,2)}}-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 5)}^{\\text{top-right corner at (0,5)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overbrace{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 2)}^{\\text{top-right corner at (0,2)}} \\\\  & =F(5,5)-F(5,2)-F(0,5)+F(0,2) \\\\  & =0.9-0.3-0.45+0.15 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186175860 1074 "Recall that $F(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/18/181df1b4d320fac30e8fc774975c8e1ae3fa6f4d360a49a38d23f1de96ebf7cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,5).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $\\color{red}(5,2)$ and ${\\color{red}{(0,5)}}.$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ${\\color{blue}{(0,2)}}.$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 5,2\\le Y\\le 5) & =\\overbrace{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 5)}^{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,5)[/math]}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 2)}^{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,2)[/math]}}-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 5)}^{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,5)[/math]}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overbrace{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 2)}^{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,2)[/math]}} \\\\  & =F(5,5)-F(5,2)-F(0,5)+F(0,2) \\\\  & =0.9-0.3-0.45+0.15 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186175869 1074 "The profit is equal to the revenue minus the cost:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (55x - 0.04x^{2}) - (610 + 45x) \\\\\n&= 55x - 0.04x^{2} - 610 - 45x \\\\\n&=-0.04x^{2} + 10x - 610\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, the maximum profit corresponds to the highest point on the graph of the profit function $P(x)$.\n\n![](images/62/623a885e58f8e45d4a13152e4883bedc6b441136cbf8b76fd6a0d36f41221a2d.png)\n\nIn this case, the profit function\n\n$$\nP(x) = -0.04x^2+10x-610\n$$\n\nis a parabola that opens downward (since the leading coefficient is negative). The highest point on the graph of $P(x)$ is the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nx_\\textrm{max}= -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nP(x) = -0.04x^2+10x-610\n$$\n\nwe have $a = -0.04$ and $b=10$, which gives\n\n$$\nx_\\textrm{max}=-\\dfrac{10}{2(-0.04)}=125\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=125$ into the profit function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(125) &= -0.04(125)^{2} + 10(125) - 610 \\\\\n&=-625 + 1250 - 610 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum profit is\n\n$$\n15 \\cdot 1\\,000\\,000 = \\$15\\,000\\,000\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186175869 1074 "The profit is equal to the revenue minus the cost:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (55x - 0.04x^{2}) - (610 + 45x) \\\\\n&= 55x - 0.04x^{2} - 610 - 45x \\\\\n&=-0.04x^{2} + 10x - 610\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, the maximum profit corresponds to the highest point on the graph of the profit function $P(x)$.\n\n![](images/62/623a885e58f8e45d4a13152e4883bedc6b441136cbf8b76fd6a0d36f41221a2d.png)\n\nIn this case, the profit function\n\n$$\nP(x) = -0.04x^2+10x-610\n$$\n\nis a parabola that opens downward (since the leading coefficient is negative). The highest point on the graph of $P(x)$ is the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nx_\\textrm{max}= -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nP(x) = -0.04x^2+10x-610\n$$\n\nwe have $a = -0.04$ and $b=10$, which gives\n\n$$\nx_\\textrm{max}=-\\dfrac{10}{2(-0.04)}=125\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=125$ into the profit function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(125) &= -0.04(125)^{2} + 10(125) - 610 \\\\\n&=-625 + 1250 - 610 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum profit is\n\n$$\n15 \\cdot 1\\,000\\,000 = \\[math]15\\,000\\,000\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186175871 1071 "A company produces $x$ units of baseball caps per month. Each unit costs $\\$1.50$ to make, and the company’s fixed costs are $\\$3\\,700$ per month. Calculate the total cost of producing $400$ units of the product." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186175871 1071 "A company produces $x$ units of baseball caps per month. Each unit costs $\\[math]1.50$ to make, and the company’s fixed costs are $\\[/math]3\\,700$ per month. Calculate the total cost of producing $400$ units of the product." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186175871 1074 "The cost function $C(x)$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\nC(x) = (\\text{fixed cost}) + (\\textrm{number of units})\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fixed costs are $\\$3\\,700$ per month, and each unit costs $\\$1.50$ to make. Therefore, the cost function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= 3700 + x \\cdot 1.5 \\\\\n&= 3700 + 1.5x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the cost of producing $400$ units, we substitute $x=400$ into $C(x)$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(400) &= 3700 + 1.5(400) \\\\\n&= 3700 + 600 \\\\\n&= 4300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it costs the company $\\$4\\,300$ to produce $400$ units." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186175871 1074 "The cost function $C(x)$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\nC(x) = (\\text{fixed cost}) + (\\textrm{number of units})\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fixed costs are $\\[math]3\\,700$ per month, and each unit costs $\\[/math]1.50$ to make. Therefore, the cost function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= 3700 + x \\cdot 1.5 \\\\\n&= 3700 + 1.5x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the cost of producing $400$ units, we substitute $x=400$ into $C(x)$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(400) &= 3700 + 1.5(400) \\\\\n&= 3700 + 600 \\\\\n&= 4300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it costs the company $\\[math]4\\,300$ to produce $400$ units." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186175884 1074 "The slope of the trend line is $-2$. Therefore:\n\n> *When we run one unit horizontally to the right, the vertical coordinate decreases (i.e., moves down) by $2$ units on average*.\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The unit along the horizontal axis is \"distance (in kilometers).\" So, one unit means $1$ kilometer.\n- The unit along the vertical axis is \"value (in thousands of dollars).\" So, $2$ units mean $\\$2\\,000$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following interpretation:\n\n> *On average, the property's market value decreases by $\\$2\\,000$ with each additional kilometer from the beach*." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186175884 1074 "The slope of the trend line is $-2$. Therefore:\n\n> *When we run one unit horizontally to the right, the vertical coordinate decreases (i.e., moves down) by $2$ units on average*.\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The unit along the horizontal axis is \"distance (in kilometers).\" So, one unit means $1$ kilometer.\n- The unit along the vertical axis is \"value (in thousands of dollars).\" So, $2$ units mean $\\[math]2\\,000$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following interpretation:\n\n> *On average, the property's market value decreases by $\\[/math]2\\,000$ with each additional kilometer from the beach*." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176289 1071 "A course grade is calculated by adding the results of a written test, oral test, and colloquium, contributing $50%$, $30%,$ and $20%,$ respectively to the final grade. \n\nThe written test results are normally distributed with a mean of $64$ and a standard deviation of $8.$ The oral test results are normally distributed with a mean of $50$ and a standard deviation of $10$, while the results of the colloquium are normally distributed with a mean of $60$ and a standard deviation of $10.$ Find the probability that a randomly selected student achieves a grade greater than $70.$ You may assume that the scores on each test are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ | $\\,0.9738\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176289 1071 "A course grade is calculated by adding the results of a written test, oral test, and colloquium, contributing $50\\%$, $30\\%,$ and $20\\%,$ respectively to the final grade. \n\nThe written test results are normally distributed with a mean of $64$ and a standard deviation of $8.$ The oral test results are normally distributed with a mean of $50$ and a standard deviation of $10$, while the results of the colloquium are normally distributed with a mean of $60$ and a standard deviation of $10.$ Find the probability that a randomly selected student achieves a grade greater than $70.$ You may assume that the scores on each test are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ | $\\,0.9738\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176289 1074 "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the written test, oral test, and colloquium results, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(64,{8}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(50,{10}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(60,{10}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the final grade of a randomly selected student. Since the written, oral, and colloquium contribute $50%$, $30%,$ and $20%$, respectively, to the final grade, we have\n\n$$\nY=0.5{X}_{1}+0.3{X}_{2}+0.2{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =0.5\\cdot 64+0.3\\cdot 50+0.2\\cdot 60 \\\\  & =59, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & =(0.5)^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}+(0.3)^{2}\\cdot {10}^{2}+(0.2)^{2}\\cdot {10}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25\\cdot 64+0.09\\cdot 10+0.04\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ∼N(59,29).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>70),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>70) & =P(Z>\\frac{70-59}{\\sqrt{29}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>2.04) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<2.04) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2.04)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(2.04)≈0.9793.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>70) & ≈1-Φ(2.04) \\\\  & ≈1-0.9793 \\\\  & =0.0207.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176289 1074 "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the written test, oral test, and colloquium results, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(64,{8}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(50,{10}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(60,{10}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the final grade of a randomly selected student. Since the written, oral, and colloquium contribute $50\\%$, $30\\%,$ and $20\\%$, respectively, to the final grade, we have\n\n$$\nY=0.5{X}_{1}+0.3{X}_{2}+0.2{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =0.5\\cdot 64+0.3\\cdot 50+0.2\\cdot 60 \\\\  & =59, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & =(0.5)^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}+(0.3)^{2}\\cdot {10}^{2}+(0.2)^{2}\\cdot {10}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25\\cdot 64+0.09\\cdot 10+0.04\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ∼N(59,29).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>70),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>70) & =P(Z>\\frac{70-59}{\\sqrt{29}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>2.04) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<2.04) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2.04)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(2.04)≈0.9793.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>70) & ≈1-Φ(2.04) \\\\  & ≈1-0.9793 \\\\  & =0.0207.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176296 1071 "A company produces $x$ units of fertilizer per month. Each unit costs $$40$ to make, and the company’s fixed costs are $$5000$ per month. Calculate the cost function $C(x)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176296 1071 "A company produces $x$ units of fertilizer per month. Each unit costs $\\$40$ to make, and the company’s fixed costs are $\\$5000$ per month. Calculate the cost function $C(x)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176340 1071 "An antique broker has records about the value of an artwork $y$ (in thousands of dollars) at certain times $x$ (in decades) since the artwork was produced. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=3.2x+4.5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the residual $5$ decades after the artwork was produced if the actual value of the artwork after $5$ decades is $$23\\,000?$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176340 1071 "An antique broker has records about the value of an artwork $y$ (in thousands of dollars) at certain times $x$ (in decades) since the artwork was produced. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=3.2x+4.5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the residual $5$ decades after the artwork was produced if the actual value of the artwork after $5$ decades is $\\$23\\,000?$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176340 1074 "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the estimated value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{y} & =3.2x+4.5 \\\\  & =3.2(5)+4.5 \\\\  & =20.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y} \\\\  & =23-20.5 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $$2\\,500.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176340 1074 "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the estimated value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{y} & =3.2x+4.5 \\\\  & =3.2(5)+4.5 \\\\  & =20.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y} \\\\  & =23-20.5 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $\\$2\\,500.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176427 1071 "A company produces $x$ kilograms of sugar per day. Each kilogram costs $$2.50$ to make, and the company’s fixed costs are $$625$ per day.\nCalculate the total cost of producing $120$ kilograms of sugar in a day.It costs the company $$$ {{field-1}} to produce $120$ kilograms of sugar in a day.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176427 1071 "A company produces $x$ kilograms of sugar per day. Each kilogram costs $\\$2.50$ to make, and the company’s fixed costs are $\\$625$ per day.\nCalculate the total cost of producing $120$ kilograms of sugar in a day.It costs the company \\$ {{field-1}} to produce $120$ kilograms of sugar in a day.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176428 1071 "To produce smartphones, a manufacturing company models the price pe