 to be $1%$ of the original number? Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176473 1071 "The rate at which the number of particles in a radioactive substance decays $N(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-0.01N.\n$$\n\nHow many years will it take for the number of particles to be $1\\%$ of the original number? Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176473 1074 "We model the number of particles using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-0.01N.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.01t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial number of particles.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of particles is $1%$ of its initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nN({t}_{1})=0.01{N}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.01{N}_{0} & ={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.01 & ={e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01) & =-0.01{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01)}{0.01} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈460.517\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $461$ years for the number of particles to be $1%$ of the original number." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176473 1074 "We model the number of particles using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-0.01N.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.01t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial number of particles.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of particles is $1\\%$ of its initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nN({t}_{1})=0.01{N}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.01{N}_{0} & ={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.01 & ={e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01) & =-0.01{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01)}{0.01} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈460.517\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $461$ years for the number of particles to be $1\\%$ of the original number." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176528 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y={1}^{2}\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y={2}^{2}\\cdot 2=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+8=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $10$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈10.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176528 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y={1}^{2}\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y={2}^{2}\\cdot 2=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+8=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $10$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈10.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176585 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{′} = 2x^{2}y, y(1) = 1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $$? |" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176585 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{′} = 2x^{2}y, y(1) = 1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | ? |" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176586 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{′} = xe^{y}, y(2) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx = 2$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $$? |" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186176586 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{′} = xe^{y}, y(2) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx = 2$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | ? |" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176964 1064 "An important function that company managers and economists often track is the **price function** of a good, usually denoted $p(x)$. The price function gives the **price per unit** of selling $x$ units of a product.\n\nWhen purchasing items in large quantities, there is often a discount which causes the price per unit to decrease as the number of units increases. This is one motivation behind introducing a price function.\n\nFor example, suppose that a textile factory manufactures pants. If the price per unit (in dollars) is given by\n\n$$\np(x) = 500-x^2\n$$\n\nthen the price for $10$ units is\n\n$$\np(10) = 500-(10)^2 = \\$400\n$$\n\nIt's important to remember that this is the price *per unit*. The total amount that the company would charge for selling $10$ units is\n\n$$\n10\\cdot p(10) = 10\\cdot \\$400 = \\$4\\,000\n$$\n\nThis example shows that the price function is closely connected to the revenue function $R(x)$. Typically, if a company sells $x$ units at a price $p(x)$, then the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the case of the above example, the revenue function $R(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= x \\cdot p(x) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot (500 - x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 500x - x^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhile the price of a good sometimes varies with $x$, other times it may be constant. In cases when the unit price is constant, you'll see the price per unit quoted as a single figure (usually in dollars)." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186176964 1064 "An important function that company managers and economists often track is the **price function** of a good, usually denoted $p(x)$. The price function gives the **price per unit** of selling $x$ units of a product.\n\nWhen purchasing items in large quantities, there is often a discount which causes the price per unit to decrease as the number of units increases. This is one motivation behind introducing a price function.\n\nFor example, suppose that a textile factory manufactures pants. If the price per unit (in dollars) is given by\n\n$$\np(x) = 500-x^2\n$$\n\nthen the price for $10$ units is\n\n$$\np(10) = 500-(10)^2 = \\[math]400\n$$\n\nIt's important to remember that this is the price *per unit*. The total amount that the company would charge for selling $10$ units is\n\n$$\n10\\cdot p(10) = 10\\cdot \\[/math]400 = \\[math]4\\,000\n$$\n\nThis example shows that the price function is closely connected to the revenue function $R(x)$. Typically, if a company sells $x$ units at a price $p(x)$, then the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the case of the above example, the revenue function $R(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= x \\cdot p(x) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot (500 - x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 500x - x^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhile the price of a good sometimes varies with $x$, other times it may be constant. In cases when the unit price is constant, you'll see the price per unit quoted as a single figure (usually in dollars)." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186177018 1064 "As an example, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis matrix has a single eigenvalue $\\lambda = {\\color{red}2}$ and, since $A$ is not scalar, it is not diagonalizable. Thus, the Jordan canonical form of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can show that $A$ has the Jordan canonical decomposition $A =  PJP^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe main focus of this lesson is to find a matrix $P$ for any non-diagonalizable $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that satisfies the equation $A = PJP^{-1}.$\n\nNotice that the columns of $P$ form a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that generates the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe vectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{DarkBlue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ are generalized eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = {\\color{red}2}.$ Here, ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ while ${\\color{DarkBlue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2.$ \n\nIn general, the matrix $P$ of any Jordan canonical decomposition of a non-diagonalizable $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda$ must satisfy the following property:\n\n- The columns of $P$ associated with a Jordan block $J_2(\\lambda)$ are generalized eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ where\n\n- the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector, and\n\n- the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ that is mapped to the first column under the action of $A-\\lambda I.$\n\n**Watch out!** The order of the columns of $P$ is important! The first column should *always* be an eigenvector." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186177018 1064 "As an example, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis matrix has a single eigenvalue $\\lambda = {\\color{red}2}$ and, since $A$ is not scalar, it is not diagonalizable. Thus, the Jordan canonical form of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can show that $A$ has the Jordan canonical decomposition $A =  PJP^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe main focus of this lesson is to find a matrix [math]P[/math] for any non-diagonalizable [math]2\\times 2[/math] matrix [math]A[/math] that satisfies the equation [math]A = PJP^{-1}.[/math]\n\nNotice that the columns of $P$ form a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that generates the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe vectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{DarkBlue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ are generalized eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = {\\color{red}2}.$ Here, ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ while ${\\color{DarkBlue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2.$ \n\nIn general, the matrix $P$ of any Jordan canonical decomposition of a non-diagonalizable $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda$ must satisfy the following property:\n\n- The columns of $P$ associated with a Jordan block $J_2(\\lambda)$ are generalized eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ where\n\n- the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector, and\n\n- the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ that is mapped to the first column under the action of $A-\\lambda I.$\n\n**Watch out!** The order of the columns of $P$ is important! The first column should *always* be an eigenvector." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186177022 1064 "We can use our knowledge of quadratic functions to maximize a profit function. A profit function $P(x)$ often takes the form of a downward-opening parabola, and the maximum profit corresponds to the vertex of the parabola.\n\n![](images/8d/8d4dda42334dcda79cfcba92996bca13d8b068dabe45353ff3969aa648255d9f.png)\n\nFor example, suppose that the daily profit $P$, in dollars, of a small business is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(x) = -3x^2 + 120x - 42\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of units of a particular product sold by the company per day. How many units should the company sell per day to maximize its profit?\n\nThe profit function is a parabola that opens downward (since the leading coefficient is negative). Therefore, the highest point on the graph of $P(x)$ is the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nx_\\textrm{max}= -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nP(x) = -3x^2 + 120x - 42\n$$\n\nwe have $a = -3$ and $b=120$, which gives\n\n$$\nx_\\textrm{max}=-\\dfrac{120}{2(-3)}=20\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its daily profit, the business should sell $20$ units per day. The maximum daily profit of this company is\n\n$$\nP(20) =-3(20)^{2} + 120(20) - 42 = \\$1158\n$$\n\nAs well as maximizing profit functions, we can also maximize revenue functions and minimize cost functions. Let's see an example." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186177022 1064 "We can use our knowledge of quadratic functions to maximize a profit function. A profit function $P(x)$ often takes the form of a downward-opening parabola, and the maximum profit corresponds to the vertex of the parabola.\n\n![](images/8d/8d4dda42334dcda79cfcba92996bca13d8b068dabe45353ff3969aa648255d9f.png)\n\nFor example, suppose that the daily profit $P$, in dollars, of a small business is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(x) = -3x^2 + 120x - 42\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of units of a particular product sold by the company per day. How many units should the company sell per day to maximize its profit?\n\nThe profit function is a parabola that opens downward (since the leading coefficient is negative). Therefore, the highest point on the graph of $P(x)$ is the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nx_\\textrm{max}= -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nP(x) = -3x^2 + 120x - 42\n$$\n\nwe have $a = -3$ and $b=120$, which gives\n\n$$\nx_\\textrm{max}=-\\dfrac{120}{2(-3)}=20\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its daily profit, the business should sell $20$ units per day. The maximum daily profit of this company is\n\n$$\nP(20) =-3(20)^{2} + 120(20) - 42 = \\[math]1158\n$$\n\nAs well as maximizing profit functions, we can also maximize revenue functions and minimize cost functions. Let's see an example." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186177024 1064 "We can construct a cost function $C(x)$ for a business using the following equation:\n\n$$\nC(x) = (\\text{fixed cost}) + (\\textrm{number of units})\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})\n$$\n\nLet's break this equation down a bit:\n\n- The **fixed cost** is the part of the cost function that does not change. Irrespective of how many units are produced, fixed costs are always the same. Fixed costs typically incorporate rent, utilities, communications, employee wages, taxes, etc.\n- The term $(\\textrm{number of units})\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})$ represents the **variable cost.** This part of the cost function changes depending on how many units are produced.\n\nFor example, suppose that a company produces $x$ units of sweaters per month. Each unit costs $\\$49$ to make, and the company's fixed costs are $\\$4\\,500$ per month.\n\nWe can calculate the cost function $C(x)$ for this company as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= (fixed cost) + (number of units) \\cdot (cost per unit) \\\\\n&= 4500 + x \\cdot 49 \\\\\n&= 4500 + 49x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the cost of producing $50$ units, we substitute $x=50$ into $C(x)$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(50) &= 4500 + 49(50) \\\\\n&= 4500 + 2450 \\\\\n&= 6950\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it costs the company $\\$6\\,950$ to produce $50$ units." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186177024 1064 "We can construct a cost function $C(x)$ for a business using the following equation:\n\n$$\nC(x) = (\\text{fixed cost}) + (\\textrm{number of units})\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})\n$$\n\nLet's break this equation down a bit:\n\n- The **fixed cost** is the part of the cost function that does not change. Irrespective of how many units are produced, fixed costs are always the same. Fixed costs typically incorporate rent, utilities, communications, employee wages, taxes, etc.\n- The term $(\\textrm{number of units})\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})$ represents the **variable cost.** This part of the cost function changes depending on how many units are produced.\n\nFor example, suppose that a company produces $x$ units of sweaters per month. Each unit costs $\\[math]49$ to make, and the company's fixed costs are $\\[/math]4\\,500$ per month.\n\nWe can calculate the cost function $C(x)$ for this company as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= (fixed cost) + (number of units) \\cdot (cost per unit) \\\\\n&= 4500 + x \\cdot 49 \\\\\n&= 4500 + 49x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the cost of producing $50$ units, we substitute $x=50$ into $C(x)$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(50) &= 4500 + 49(50) \\\\\n&= 4500 + 2450 \\\\\n&= 6950\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it costs the company $\\[math]6\\,950$ to produce $50$ units." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186177025 1064 "A company manufactures canned mixed nuts. The price function for this company is given by\n\n$$\np(x) = 60-0.01x\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced and sold per month. Additionally, each unit costs $\\$25$ to make, and the company's fixed costs are $\\$8\\,800$ per month.\n\nLet's calculate the profit function $P(x)$ for this company.\n\nRecall that profit function $P(x)$ is equal to the revenue function $R(x)$ minus the cost function $C(x)$:\n\n$$\nP(x)=R(x)-C(x)\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the profit function, we first need to construct $R(x)$ and $C(x)$.\n\n- We're given the price function $p(x)$, so the revenue function is\n$$\n\\displaystyle R(x)|= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit); = x \\cdot p(x); = x(60 - 0.01x); = 60x - 0.01x^{2}.\n$$\n- We're also given that the fixed costs are $\\$8\\,800$ per month and that each unit costs $\\$25$ to make. Therefore, the cost function is\n$$\n\\displaystyle C(x)|= (fixed cost) + (number of units) \\cdot (cost per unit); = 8800 + x \\cdot 25; = 8800 + 25x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now calculate the profit function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (60x - 0.01x^{2}) - (8800 + 25x) \\\\\n&= 60x - 0.01x^{2} - 8800 - 25x \\\\\n&=-0.01x^{2} + 35x - 8800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186177025 1064 "A company manufactures canned mixed nuts. The price function for this company is given by\n\n$$\np(x) = 60-0.01x\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced and sold per month. Additionally, each unit costs $\\[math]25$ to make, and the company's fixed costs are $\\[/math]8\\,800$ per month.\n\nLet's calculate the profit function $P(x)$ for this company.\n\nRecall that profit function $P(x)$ is equal to the revenue function $R(x)$ minus the cost function $C(x)$:\n\n$$\nP(x)=R(x)-C(x)\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the profit function, we first need to construct $R(x)$ and $C(x)$.\n\n- We're given the price function $p(x)$, so the revenue function is\n$$\n\\displaystyle R(x)|= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit); = x \\cdot p(x); = x(60 - 0.01x); = 60x - 0.01x^{2}.\n$$\n- We're also given that the fixed costs are $\\[math]8\\,800$ per month and that each unit costs $\\[/math]25$ to make. Therefore, the cost function is\n$$\n\\displaystyle C(x)|= (fixed cost) + (number of units) \\cdot (cost per unit); = 8800 + x \\cdot 25; = 8800 + 25x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now calculate the profit function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (60x - 0.01x^{2}) - (8800 + 25x) \\\\\n&= 60x - 0.01x^{2} - 8800 - 25x \\\\\n&=-0.01x^{2} + 35x - 8800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186177047 1064 "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model $\\widehat{y} =f(x),$ the **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-f(x) \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value.\n\nTo illustrate this concept graphically, consider the following example.\n\nThe owner of an ice cream shop keeps track of the average daily temperature $x$ (measured in $^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$) and the resulting daily ice cream sales $y$ (in dollars). The scatter plot for several randomly selected days and the corresponding linear regression line are shown below. \n\n![](images/fc/fc149b8d911a7a9863df9cc25c635d6155accd676e83e335a65d9083596b779b.png)\n\nSuppose the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny= 2.5 x +165.\n$$\n\nLet's use our regression line to compute the residual of the point with coordinates $\\color{red}(82, 360)$ (shown in red on our graph). \n\nFirst, notice that since the highlighted data point has coordinates ${\\color{red}(82, 360)},$ our actual value is $y = {\\color{red}360}.$\n\nNext, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{y} & =2.5x+165 \\\\  & =2.5(82)+165 \\\\  & =370\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/38/3843c71b50251ae035378507ffb22cfade48dff8e1544d8e1317e4e6d611f7a1.png)\n\nFinally, we compute the residual: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y} \\\\  & =360-370 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nThe residual tells us that the linear model *overestimates* the true volume of ice cream sales by $\\$10$ when the outside temperature is $82^\\circ\\,\\textrm{F}.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186177047 1064 "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model $\\widehat{y} =f(x),$ the **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-f(x) \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value.\n\nTo illustrate this concept graphically, consider the following example.\n\nThe owner of an ice cream shop keeps track of the average daily temperature $x$ (measured in $^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$) and the resulting daily ice cream sales $y$ (in dollars). The scatter plot for several randomly selected days and the corresponding linear regression line are shown below. \n\n![](images/fc/fc149b8d911a7a9863df9cc25c635d6155accd676e83e335a65d9083596b779b.png)\n\nSuppose the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny= 2.5 x +165.\n$$\n\nLet's use our regression line to compute the residual of the point with coordinates $\\color{red}(82, 360)$ (shown in red on our graph). \n\nFirst, notice that since the highlighted data point has coordinates ${\\color{red}(82, 360)},$ our actual value is $y = {\\color{red}360}.$\n\nNext, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{y} & =2.5x+165 \\\\  & =2.5(82)+165 \\\\  & =370\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/38/3843c71b50251ae035378507ffb22cfade48dff8e1544d8e1317e4e6d611f7a1.png)\n\nFinally, we compute the residual: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y} \\\\  & =360-370 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nThe residual tells us that the linear model *overestimates* the true volume of ice cream sales by $\\[math]10$ when the outside temperature is $82^\\circ\\,\\textrm{F}.$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186179558 1071 "Due to high performance, Anna's weekly bonus, which started at $\\$150$, increases by $3\\%$ every week. Let $B(t)$ be Anna's bonus after $t$ weeks. Is $B(t)$ an exponential or a linear function?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186179558 1071 "Due to high performance, Anna's weekly bonus, which started at $\\[math]150$, increases by $3\\%$ every week. Let $B(t)$ be Anna's bonus after $t$ weeks. Is $B(t)$ an exponential or a linear function?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186179558 1074 "Anna's weekly bonus started at $\\$150$, increasing by $3\\%$ each week. Since $3\\%$ as a decimal is $0.03$, an increase of $3\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.03 = 1.03\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n- After $1$ week, Anna's bonus will be $150\\cdot (1.03)$ dollars.\n- After $\\color{blue}2$ weeks, Anna's bonus will be $150\\cdot (1.03)^{\\color{blue}{2}}$ dollars.\n- After $\\color{blue}3$ weeks, Anna's bonus will be $150\\cdot (1.03)^{\\color{blue}{3}}$ dollars.$\\quad\\vdots$\n- After $\\color{blue}t$ weeks, Anna's bonus will be $150\\cdot(1.03)^{\\color{blue}{t}}$ dollars.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB(t) = 150\\cdot(1.03)^t\n$$\n\nwhich is an exponential growth function.\n\nFinally, we conclude that $B(t)$ is exponential because it increases by a factor of $1.03$ when $t$ increases by $1$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186179558 1074 "Anna's weekly bonus started at $\\[math]150$, increasing by $3\\%$ each week. Since $3\\%$ as a decimal is $0.03$, an increase of $3\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.03 = 1.03\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n- After $1$ week, Anna's bonus will be $150\\cdot (1.03)$ dollars.\n- After $\\color{blue}2$ weeks, Anna's bonus will be $150\\cdot (1.03)^{\\color{blue}{2}}$ dollars.\n- After $\\color{blue}3$ weeks, Anna's bonus will be $150\\cdot (1.03)^{\\color{blue}{3}}$ dollars.$\\quad\\vdots$\n- After $\\color{blue}t$ weeks, Anna's bonus will be $150\\cdot(1.03)^{\\color{blue}{t}}$ dollars.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB(t) = 150\\cdot(1.03)^t\n$$\n\nwhich is an exponential growth function.\n\nFinally, we conclude that $B(t)$ is exponential because it increases by a factor of $1.03$ when $t$ increases by $1$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186179565 1071 "Monica pays $\\$3\\,500$ into an investment account at the beginning of every year. She earns an annual interest of $2\\%$, paid at the end of the year. To the nearest dollar, how much money will be in her investment account by the end of the $9$th year?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186179565 1071 "Monica pays $\\[math]3\\,500$ into an investment account at the beginning of every year. She earns an annual interest of $2\\%$, paid at the end of the year. To the nearest dollar, how much money will be in her investment account by the end of the $9$th year?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186179565 1074 "Let $S_n$ be the value of Monica's investment at the end of the $n$th year.\n\n- At the end of the year $1$, Monica's investment will be worth\n$S_1 = \\$3\\,50