nt at the end of the $n$th year.\n\n- The interest rate is $4.8 \\%$, compounded monthly. Therefore, at the end of year $1$, Charlie's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_1 = \\[math]1\\,000\\cdot \\left(1 + \\dfrac{0.048}{12}\\right)^{12} = \\[/math]1\\,000 \\cdot \\left(1.004\\right)^{12}.\n$$\n- To calculate the value of the investment at the end of year $2$, we need to add $\\[math]1\\,000$ to the balance at the end of year $1$, and then increase the entire amount by $4.8 \\%$, compounded monthly. Therefore, at the end of year $2$, Charlie's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{2}|= (S_{1} + [/math]1000) \\cdot (1.004)^{12}; = (\\[math]1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} + \\[/math]1000) \\cdot (1.004)^{12}; = \\[math]1000 \\cdot [(1.004)^{12}]^{2} + \\[/math]1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12}.\n$$\n- $\\quad \\vdots$\n- Continuing this process, we may deduce that by the end of year $6$, Charlie's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{6}|= \\[math]1000 \\cdot [(1.004)^{12}]^{6} + \\[/math]1000 \\cdot [(1.004)^{12}]^{5} + ⋯ + \\[math]1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12}; = \\[/math]1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\cdot {[(1.004)^{12}]^{5} + [(1.004)^{12}]^{4} + ⋯ + [(1.004)^{12}] + 1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression inside the curly braces is a geometric series with $a_1 = 1$,\n\n$$\nr = (1.004)^{12}\n$$\n\nand $N = 6$. Thus, after $6$ years, Charlie's investment will be worth\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{6} &= \\[math]1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\cdot ((a_{1}(1 - r^{N}))/(1 - r)) \\\\\n&= \\[/math]1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\cdot ((1 \\cdot (1 - [(1.004)^{12}]^{6}))/(1 - (1.004)^{12})) \\\\\n&= \\[math]1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\cdot ((1 - [(1.004)^{12}]^{6})/(1 - (1.004)^{12})) \\\\\n&\\approx \\[/math]7119\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest dollar." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186179567 1071 "Harold pays $\\$2\\,500$ into an investment account at the beginning of every year. He earns an annual interest of $4\\%$, compounded continuously. To th