 is smaller than Tom's mean value.\n3. Ben and Tom calculated the same value for the mean absolute deviation (MAD)." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186180311 1071 "John pays $$2000$ into an investment account at the beginning of every year. He earns an annual interest of $3 \\mathrm{\\% } ,$ paid at the end of the year. To the nearest dollar, how much money will be in his investment account by the end of the $8$th year?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186180311 1071 "John pays $\\$2000$ into an investment account at the beginning of every year. He earns an annual interest of $3 \\mathrm{\\% } ,$ paid at the end of the year. To the nearest dollar, how much money will be in his investment account by the end of the $8$th year?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186180694 1064 "An investor plans to retire in $30$ years, and they wish to build up enough savings to live on throughout their retirement. So they decide to place $\\$5\\,000$ into an investment account that generates a return of $8\\%$, compounded annually.\n\nThe value $S_n$ of this investment after $n$ years is given by the exponential growth expression\n\n$$\nS_n = \\$5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)^n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of this investment in $30$ years is\n\n$$\nS_{30} = \\$5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)^{30} \\approx \\$50\\,313\n$$\n\nWhile $\\$50\\,313$ might seem like a lot of money, it is unlikely to be enough to cover the investor's retirement.\n\nIn reality, it's unlikely that an investor will make a single deposit into an investment account and leave it there for $30$ years. Instead, they are more likely to make multiple deposits throughout their life.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn how geometric series can be used to calculate the value of an investment portfolio when an investor makes multiple deposits." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186180694 1064 "An investor plans to retire in $30$ years, and they wish to build up enough savings to live on throughout their retirement. So they decide to place $\\[math]5\\,000$ into an investment account that generates a return of $8\\%$, compounded annually.\n\nThe value $S_n$ of this investment after $n$ years is given by the exponential growth expression\n\n$$\nS_n = \\[/math]5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)^n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of this investment in $30$ years is\n\n$$\nS_{30} = \\[math]5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)^{30} \\approx \\[/math]50\\,313\n$$\n\nWhile $\\[math]50\\,313$ might seem like a lot of money, it is unlikely to be enough to cover the investor's retirement.\n\nIn reality, it's unlikely that an investor will make a single deposit into an investment account and leave it there for $30$ years. Instead, they are more likely to make multiple deposits throughout their life.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn how geometric series can be used to calculate the value of an investment portfolio when an investor makes multiple deposits." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186180695 1064 "Now let's suppose that the investor pays $\\$5\\,000$ *per year* into an investment account that generates a return of $8\\%$, compounded annually. Let $S_n$ be the value of the investment at the end of the $n$th year. Our goal is to calculate $S_{30}$, the value of the portfolio when the investor retires in $30$ years.\n\nLet's work out $S_n$ for\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3,\\ldots\n$$\n\nand see if we can spot a pattern.\n\n- At the end of year $1$, the investment will be worth\n$S_1 = \\$5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)$.\n- To calculate the value of the investment at the end of year $2$, we first need to add $\\$5\\,000$ to the balance at the end of year $1$. This gives the expression\n$S_1 + 5\\,000$.\nThen, we increase the amount by $8 \\%$. Therefore, at the end of the year $2$, the investment will be worth\n$S_2 = \\big(S_1 + 5\\,000\\big) \\cdot (1.08)$.\nSubstituting our expression for $S_1$ and expanding the parentheses, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{2}|= (\\$5000 \\cdot (1.08) + \\$5000) \\cdot (1.08); = \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{2} + \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08).\n$$\n- Similarly, at the end of the year $3$, the investment will be worth\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{3}|= (S_{2} + \\$5000) \\cdot (1.08); = [(\\$5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{2} + \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08)) + \\$5000] \\cdot (1.08); = \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{3} + \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{2} + \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08).\n$$\n\nYou can probably spot the pattern now. We can deduce that the expression for $S_{30}$ is equal to\n\n$$\nS_{30} = \\$5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)^{30} + \\$5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)^{29} + \\cdots + {5 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\cdot  ( 1.08 {)}^{2} +}5\\,000\\cdot (1.08).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this sum, we first factor it as follows:\n\n$$\nS_{30} = \\$5\\,000\\cdot (1.08) \\cdot \\left[ (1.08)^{29} +(1.08)^{28} + \\cdots + (1.08) + 1 \\right]\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression inside the square brackets is a geometric series with $a_1 = 1$, $r = 1.08$, and $N = 30$. Thus, after $30$ years, the investment will be worth\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{30} &= \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08) \\cdot ((a_{1}(1 - r^{N}))/(1 - r)) \\\\\n&= \\$5400 \\cdot ((1 \\cdot (1 - 1.08^{30}))/(1 - 1.08)) \\\\\n&= \\$5400 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 - 1.08^{30}}{1 - 1.08}\\right) \\\\\n&\\approx \\$611729\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest dollar.\n\nThis example illustrates how compound interest can be used to fund retirements. The investor deposited a total of\n\n$$\n\\$5\\,000\\cdot 30 = \\$150\\,000\n$$\n\nover $30$ years, yet this was worth $\\$611\\,729$ by the time they retired!" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186180695 1064 "Now let's suppose that the investor pays $\\[math]5\\,000$ *per year* into an investment account that generates a return of $8\\%$, compounded annually. Let $S_n$ be the value of the investment at the end of the $n$th year. Our goal is to calculate $S_{30}$, the value of the portfolio when the investor retires in $30$