 parentheses:\n$f \\mid ≡(x_{1}x_{2}⊕x_{1})⊕x_{1}x_{2}; ≡x_{1}x_{2}⊕x_{1}⊕x_{1}x_{2}$\n- Using the fact that $A \\oplus B \\equiv B \\oplus A$, we rearrange to pair up identical terms:\n$f \\mid ≡x_{1}x_{2}⊕x_{1}x_{2}⊕x_{1}$\n- Using the fact that $A \\oplus A \\equiv 0$ and $0 \\oplus A \\equiv A$, we cancel out the pair of identical terms:\n$f \\mid ≡x_{1}x_{2}⊕x_{1}x_{2}⊕x_{1}; ≡0⊕x_{1}; ≡x_{1}$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184042 1074 "The linear function that models the number of smart sensors, $S$, for $t$ years after $2014$, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= mt + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope, and $b$ is the $S$-intercept.\n\nWe have two known points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nfor 2014:(t_{1}, S_{1}) &= (0, 4500) \\\\\nfor 2021:(t_{2}, S_{2}) &= (7, 10500)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe first find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{S_{2} - S_{1}}{t_{2} - t_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10, 500 - 4, 500}{7 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6, 000}{7} \\\\\n&\\approx 857.14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since the number of sensors in $2014$ (when $t = 0$) is $4,500$, we have $b = 4,500$. So we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= mt + b \\\\\n&= 857.14t + 4, 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184042 1074 "The linear function that models the number of smart sensors, $S$, for $t$ years after $2014$, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= mt + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope, and $b$ is the $S$-intercept.\n\nWe have two known points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nfor [math]2014[/math]:(t_{1}, S_{1}) &= (0, 4500) \\\\\nfor [math]2021[/math]:(t_{2}, S_{2}) &= (7, 10500)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe first find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{S_{2} - S_{1}}{t_{2} - t_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10, 500 - 4, 500}{7 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6, 000}{7} \\\\\n&\\approx 857.14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since the number of sensors in $2014$ (when $t = 0$) is $4,500$, we have $b = 4,500$. So we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= mt + b \\\\\n&= 857.14t + 4, 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184043 1071 "A phone company modeled the number of minutes $M$ used by a customer each month as a linear function of the monthly charge. A customer used $1{,}200$ minutes when the monthly charge was $\\$40$, and $2{,}400$ minutes when the charge was $\\$70$. Based on this model, what monthly charge would correspond to $1{,}800$ minutes of usage?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184043 1071 "A phone company modeled the number of minutes $M$ used by a customer each month as a linear function of the monthly charge. A customer used $1{,}200$ minutes when the monthly charge was $\\[math]40$, and $2{,}400$ minutes when the charge was $\\[/math]70$. Based on this model, what monthly charge would correspond to $1{,}800$ minutes of usage?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184043 1074 "The linear equation that models the number of minutes $M$, in minutes, as a function of the monthly charge $c$, in dollars, is\n\n$$\nM = mc + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope, and $b$ is the $M$-intercept.\n\nWe have two known data points:\n\n$$\n(c_{1}, M_{1}) = (40, 1200); (c_{2}, M_{2}) = (70, 2400)\n$$\n\nWe first find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{M_{2} - M_{1}}{c_{2} - c_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2,400 - 1,200}{70 - 40} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1,200}{30} \\\\\n&= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the usage function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= mc + b \\\\\n&= 40c + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use one of the given points, say $(70, 2\\,400)$, to find the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2,400 &= 40 \\cdot 70 + b \\\\\n2,400 &= 2,800 + b \\\\\nb &= -400\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\nM = 40c - 400\n$$\n\nTo find the charge when\n\n$$\nM = 1{,}800\n$$\n\nwe substitute and solve for $c{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1,800 &= 40c - 400 \\\\\n1,800 + 400 &= 40c - 400 + 400 \\\\\n2,200 &= 40c \\\\\nc &= \\frac{2,200}{40} \\\\\nc &= 55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the monthly charge is $\\$55$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184043 1074 "The linear equation that models the number of minutes $M$, in minutes, as a function of the monthly charge $c$, in dollars, is\n\n$$\nM = mc + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope, and $b$ is the $M$-intercept.\n\nWe have two known data points:\n\n$$\n(c_{1}, M_{1}) = (40, 1200); (c_{2}, M_{2}) = (70, 2400)\n$$\n\nWe first find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{M_{2} - M_{1}}{c_{2} - c_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2,400 - 1,200}{70 - 40} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1,200}{30} \\\\\n&= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the usage function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= mc + b \\\\\n&= 40c + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use one of the given points, say $(70, 2\\,400)$, to find the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2,400 &= 40 \\cdot 70 + b \\\\\n2,400 &= 2,800 + b \\\\\nb &= -400\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\nM = 40c - 400\n$$\n\nTo find the charge when\n\n$$\nM = 1{,}800\n$$\n\nwe substitute and solve for $c{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1,800 &= 40c - 400 \\\\\n1,800 + 400 &= 40c - 400 + 400 \\\\\n2,200 &= 40c \\\\\nc &= \\frac{2,200}{40} \\\\\nc &= 55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the monthly charge is $\\[math]55$." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184065 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\textrm A1] - [\\textrm A8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{w}$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add -\\mathbf{w} to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184065 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\textrm A1] - [\\textrm A8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{w}$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add [math]-\\mathbf{w}[/math] to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184065 1074 "Using the axioms of a vector space, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add -\\mathbf{w} to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom {\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom {\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184065 1074 "Using the axioms of a vector space, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add [math]-\\mathbf{w}[/math] to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom [math]{\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}[/math]}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom [math]{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}[/math]}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184100 1071 "Jenny has a hot dog stand in a park, where she makes a profit of $\\$3$ per hot dog. She needs to make a profit of at least $\\$540$ per week to continue operating. How many hot dogs does Jenny need to sell weekly to continue working?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184100 1071 "Jenny has a hot dog stand in a park, where she makes a profit of $\\[math]3$ per hot dog. She needs to make a profit of at least $\\[/math]540$ per week to continue operating. How many hot dogs does Jenny need to sell weekly to continue working?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184100 1074 "Jenny charges $\\$ 3$ per hot dog. So, if she sells $H$ hot dogs, she makes a total of $3H$ dollars.\n\nSince Jenny must make *at least* $\\$ 540$, the following inequality must be satisfied:\n\n$$\n3H \\geq 540\n$$\n\nTo solve for $H$, we divide both sides by $3$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 3 H & \\displaystyle  \\ge  540 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 H}{3}} & \\displaystyle  \\ge  {\\displaystyle \\frac{540}{3}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{3} H}{\\cancel{3}}} & \\displaystyle  \\ge  180 \\\\ \\displaystyle H & \\displaystyle  \\ge  180\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, to continue operating, Jenny must sell *at least* $180$ hot dogs." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184100 1074 "Jenny charges $\\[math] 3$ per hot dog. So, if she sells $H$ hot dogs, she makes a total of $3H$ dollars.\n\nSince Jenny must make *at least* $\\[/math] 540$, the following inequality must be satisfied:\n\n$$\n3H \\geq 540\n$$\n\nTo solve for $H$, we divide both sides by $3$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 3 H & \\displaystyle  \\ge  540 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 H}{3}} & \\displaystyle  \\ge  {\\displaystyle \\frac{540}{3}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{3} H}{\\cancel{3}}} & \\displaystyle  \\ge  180 \\\\ \\displaystyle H & \\displaystyle  \\ge  180\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, to continue operating, Jenny must sell *at least* $180$ hot dogs." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184101 1071 "Erika wants to spend at least $\\$ 115$ on a pair of sports shoes. If Erika has a job where she earns $\\$14$ for each full hour worked, how many full hours must Erika work to buy a pair of sports shoes?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184101 1071 "Erika wants to spend at least $\\[math] 115$ on a pair of sports shoes. If Erika has a job where she earns $\\[/math]14$ for each full hour worked, how many full hours must Erika work to buy a pair of sports shoes?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184101 1074 "Erika earns $\\$14$ for each full hour worked. So if she works $H$ full hours, she makes $14H$ dollars.\n\nSince she wants to spend *at least* $\\$ 115$ on the shoes, the following inequality must be satisfied:\n\n$$\n14H \\ge 115\n$$\n\nTo solve for $H$, we divide both sides of the inequality by $14{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 14 H & \\displaystyle  \\ge  115 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{14 H}{14}} & \\displaystyle  \\ge  {\\displaystyle \\frac{115}{14}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{14} H}{\\cancel{14}}} & \\displaystyle  \\ge  {\\displaystyle \\frac{115}{14}} \\\\ \\displaystyle H & \\displaystyle  \\ge  {\\displaystyle \\frac{115}{14}} \\\\ \\displaystyle H & \\displaystyle  \\ge  8 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{14}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, $H$ must be an integer. Therefore, we conclude that Erika must work at least $9$ full hours." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184101 1074 "Erika earns $\\[math]14$ for each full hour worked. So if she works $H$ full hours, she makes $14H$ dollars.\n\nSince she wants to spend *at least* $\\[/math] 115$ on the shoes, the following inequality must be satisfied:\n\n$$\n14H \\ge 115\n$$\n\nTo solve for $H$, we divide both sides of the inequality by $14{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 14 H & \\displaystyle  \\ge  115 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{14 H}{14}} & \\displaystyle  \\ge  {\\displaystyle \\frac{115}{14}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{14} H}{\\cancel{14}}} & \\displaystyle  \\ge  {\\displaystyle \\frac{115}{14}} \\\\ \\displaystyle H & \\displaystyle  \\ge  {\\displaystyle \\frac{115}{14}} \\\\ \\displaystyle H & \\displaystyle  \\ge  8 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{14}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, $H$ must be an integer. Therefore, we conclude that Erika must work at least $9$ full hours." 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184102 1071 "A box of nails costs $\\$ 8$. How many boxes of nails a person can buy for less than $\\$ 440$?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184102 1071 "A box of nails costs $\\[math] 8$. How many boxes of nails a person can buy for less than $\\[/math] 440$?" 13194139539458 17592186576411 false] [17592186184102 1074 "To buy $B$ boxes of nails, the person needs to spend $8B$ dollars.\n\nSince the person has *less than* $\\$ 440$, the following inequality must be satisfied:\n$8B \\lt 440$\n\nTo solve for $B$, we divide both sides of the inequality by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 8 B & \\displaystyle  < 440 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{8 B}{8}} & \\displaystyle  < {\\displaystyle \\frac{440}{8}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{8} B}{\\cancel{8}}} & \\displaystyle  < 55 \\\\ \\displaystyle B & \\displaystyle  < 55\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThus, the person can buy *less than* $55$ boxes of nails." 13194139539458 17592186576411 true] [17592186184102 1074 "To buy $B$ boxes of na